    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (85.34) stands as our clear anchor. Facing Kohl Drake, who carries a shaky 6.26 xERA, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from a massive 19% SP skill boost, making him our highest-projected asset for both power and counting stats. **Bryce Harper** (78.75) is our second-most potent weapon; he checks every box today, including a beneficial wind direction and an ideal #3 spot in the order against Shane Baz. Meanwhile, **Tyler Freeman** (71.80) offers sneaky value; while his raw base is lower, his high-tier SP skill adjustment and favorable park factors make him a high-efficiency play despite the lower base threshold. We are also leaning into **Jonathan Aranda** (63.92) and **Luis Arraez** (62.96), both of whom capitalize on platoon advantages and favorable lineup placement to provide stability in our AVG and R categories.\n\nOur most difficult decision came at the expense of **Kyle Karros**, who narrowly missed the starting cut with a 62.64 score. While **Kyle Karros** benefits from the same high-run environment and favorable matchup against Luinder Avila as **Tyler Freeman**, the optimizer favored the latter's slightly higher projected floor and positional flexibility. We are also keeping a watchful eye on **Lawrence Butler** (56.38); despite a solid park factor, his 13% penalty against the southpaw Framber Valdez keeps his output in the marginal range. We have opted to bench **Daulton Varsho** (49.39) and **Gleyber Torres** (47.64) today, as their projected platoon struggles\u2014particularly the 13% drag on **Gleyber Torres**\u2014make them poor risks relative to our starting nine.\n\nA primary situational risk remains the \"Lineup Pending\" status for most of the roster; we have run these models based on historical batting order trends, but late defensive switches could impact the projected R and RBI potential for players like **Cal Raleigh** or **Geraldo Perdomo**. Furthermore, **Dylan Beavers** 